        "contenuto_esteso": "To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. Iran claims Lebanon 'main topic' at peace talks with US Middle East Issued on: 21/06/2026 - 11:33 Modified: 21/06/2026 - 11:36 The United States and Iran are set to start a new round of talks in Switzerland on Sunday in a bid to finalise the Memorandum of Understanding signed last week. The teams have a timeframe of 60 days to resolve some of the more difficult issues such as what will happen to Iran's nuclear program and stockpiles of uranium. However the talks have already been shrouded in uncertainty as Israel and Hezbollah - not parties to the agreement - have continued to clash in Lebanon, with Tel Aviv refusing to withdraw from the territory. Story by Eliza Herbert and details by FRANCE 24 correspondent in Geneva, John Zarocostas. US-Iran peace talks to begin, after Tehran closed Strait of Hormuz 21/06/2026 Middle East Iran closes Strait of Hormuz despite ceasefire after continued Israeli strikes in Lebanon 20/06/2026 Middle East Israel strikes Lebanon despite ceasefire, at least 16 killed 20/06/2026 Middle East NGOs in Lebanon denounce Israeli attacks on medics amid ceasefire 20/06/2026 Middle East Could Israel lose Donald Trump’s support by continuing to drop bombs in Lebanon? 20/06/2026 Middle East Hezbollah-Israel flare-up in Lebanon despite ceasefire announced by the USA 20/06/2026 Middle East US-Iran ceasefire negociations underway in Switzerland 20/06/2026 Middle East Residents flee as Israeli strikes target southern Lebanon 20/06/2026 Middle East Israel and Hezbollah say they agree to truce despite renewed attacks 19/06/2026 Middle East"
